.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt BSDNETWORK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDNetwork
.Nd capability-mode kernel NetworkCmp provider
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the initial base-system provider for
.Dq system.Network .
It is installed in
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Network.cap
and launched only by
.Xr switchboard 8 .
.Pp
The base provider is a connection broker.
It implements bounded DNS and TCP/UDP
.Em connect
operations directly on the kernel network stack from within capability mode,
and returns the resulting connected socket to the consumer rather than proxying
its data.
Each consumer receives a private capability-mode worker enlisted in its
coalition.
The worker cannot fork, execute, or receive unrelated descriptors.
Network and PF session workers display their role and the authenticated client
label in
.Xr ps 1 .
The displayed title is diagnostic and does not grant authority.
Network-session worker startup uses a five-second status-and-release handshake;
a stalled or failed worker is terminated and reaped before admission fails.
The broker permits at most 4096 session workers in total and 64 per
authenticated client label, shared across the Network, Network.Admin and PF
endpoints.
Excess connections are closed before a worker or device descriptor is created.
A worker exit returns its admission slot.
Before acknowledging shutdown, the broker terminates and reaps its tracked
workers; a teardown failure prevents a successful shutdown acknowledgement.
Parent process descriptors are locked against transfer and inheritance by
later workers.
Name resolution runs in-process.
The parent reads
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf
through the filesystem provider and periodically publishes nameserver updates
to existing workers.
The parent also obtains
.Pa /etc/hosts
and
.Pa /etc/services
through that provider and publishes one-time shared snapshots, bounded to
64 KiB minus one byte and 1 MiB minus one byte respectively.
Workers retain read-only access to these snapshots; brokered source descriptors
are closed before they can be consumed by a single worker fork.
An initially unavailable file is retried by the parent, and a successful
snapshot becomes visible to workers that already exist.
Later edits to a successfully captured hosts or services file require provider
replacement to refresh the snapshot.
No Casper channel is involved and nothing resolution-related crosses into the
consumer.
.Pp
On a
.Em connect
or
.Em udp
request the worker creates a socket, connects it under the session policy, and
sends the connected descriptor back over the session channel with
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS .
Before delivery the descriptor is limited with
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2
to only the data-path rights
.Pq read, write, event, shutdown, get/set socket option, fcntl, fstat, and ioctl ,
with
.Xr cap_ioctls_limit 2
restricting ioctls to
.Dv FIONREAD , FIONBIO ,
and
.Dv FIOASYNC ,
.Xr cap_fcntls_limit 2
restricting fcntl to
.Dv F_GETFL
and
.Dv F_SETFL ,
and its transfer state latched so it can be delivered exactly once.
The delivered descriptor cannot bind, connect, listen, accept, or peel off.
The consumer owns all subsequent I/O on it.
The provider retains no socket state after delivery.
.Pp
A
.Em connect
request may carry a
.Va timeout_ms
bound on the connect attempt.
A value of zero, the default, keeps the historical fully-blocking
.Xr connect 2 ;
a nonzero value bounds the wait, and a connect that has not completed when the
deadline expires fails with
.Er ETIMEDOUT .
The bound applies to TCP
.Em connect
only; a
.Em udp
request ignores it.
The protocol exposes DNS, connect, udp and listen.
.Pp
.Dq system.Network ,
.Dq system.Network.Admin
and
.Dq system.Network.State
speak the FIDL library
.Sy bsd.network.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/networkcmp.fidl ;
see
.Xr fidl 7 .
A session negotiates the wire version first.
A request before negotiation, an endpoint with a prefix or nonzero IPv4 tail
bytes, a resolve request outside the historical bounds, an unknown method, a
malformed frame, a pre-FIDL record or an attached descriptor ends the session
as a protocol violation, audited as
.Dq malformed-request .
.Dq system.Network.PF
keeps its single pre-FIDL request.
.Pp
The same daemon also serves
.Dq system.Network.PF .
SwitchBoard admits only clients anointed with
.Dq system.network.pf.read ;
the daemon additionally requires the PF read session right.
For each admitted session it obtains an exact
.Pa /dev/pf
descriptor from
.Xr BSDFilesystem 8
and passes it to a private worker.
The descriptor is limited to the read-only PF state export ioctl and one
transfer to the client; the client cannot change PF rules or settings through
it.
The daemon itself runs as the unprivileged
.Dq capability
user and does not hold a directory descriptor for
.Pa /dev .
.Pp
Resolver requests cap host and service lengths, flags, address families, and
result counts.
Among the accepted resolver flags,
.Dv AI_V4MAPPED
and
.Dv AI_ALL
follow RFC 3493 for an
.Dv AF_INET6
query: when no native
.Sy AAAA
record is found, or when
.Dv AI_ALL
is also set,
.Sy A
records are fetched and appended as IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses.
.Dv AI_ADDRCONFIG
is unsupported because no configured source-address family snapshot is
available.
The compatibility API returns
.Dv EAI_BADFLAGS ;
the provider rejects the corresponding wire flag with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
without terminating the session.
It never reads resolver configuration from the consumer filesystem.
DNS queries use UDP first and retry truncated replies over TCP to the same
configured nameserver, with a bounded TCP exchange.
Resolution runs on a dedicated thread that issues its own bounded DNS queries,
so a slow resolver cannot stop socket operations on the session.
At most one resolution is active per private session; an overlapping request
fails with
.Er EBUSY .
If a client abandons or times out a request, the channel reply token prevents
the eventual late result from being mistaken for another reply.
The provider also enforces a 30-second resolver deadline.
Because the resolver interface does not provide safe in-thread cancellation,
exceeding that deadline terminates only the consumer's private NetworkCmp
worker; the typed client observes peer death and may recover according to its
component lifecycle policy.
This fail-closed boundary prevents a stuck resolver from retaining resources
or making every later resolve return
.Er EBUSY
indefinitely.
.Pp
The component bootstrap carries no client-selected policy object.
The session policy is resolved once at session creation and is immutable
thereafter; the client never supplies policy on a request.
A session whose granted channel carries the administrative right receives the
full policy, including internal destinations; every other session is scoped by
the provider's per-client policy configuration described below, keyed by the
unforgeable switchboard manifest label.
Listener authority is not part of the client API.
Socket options are not restricted by the broker: the consumer sets them itself
on the delivered descriptor within the delegated
.Dv CAP_SETSOCKOPT
right.
.Sh POLICY CONFIGURATION
Per-client policy is read once at startup, before capability mode, from
.Pa Config/bsdnetwork.conf
inside the provider's unit directory
.Pq Ev CAPABILITY_UNIT_DIR ,
that is
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Network.cap/Units/bsdnetwork.unit/Config/bsdnetwork.conf .
The file is UCL:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
default {
	resolve = true;
	connect = true;
	udp = true;
	inet4 = true;
	inet6 = true;
	internal = false;
}
clients {
	"org.example.render" { connect = false; udp = false; }
	"org.example.metrics" { internal = true; }
}
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ic default
block applies to any client label without a
.Ic clients
entry; a
.Ic clients
entry names only the dimensions it overrides and inherits the rest from
.Ic default .
Every dimension is boolean:
.Ic resolve
(DNS),
.Ic connect
(TCP),
.Ic udp
(connected datagram),
.Ic inet4
and
.Ic inet6
(address families), and
.Ic internal
(loopback, link-local, and RFC1918/ULA destinations, denied by default; this
is the SSRF guard).
A label whose resolved policy permits no operation is refused a session with
.Er EACCES .
Sessions whose channel carries the administrative right bypass this table
entirely.
.Pp
The shipped default equals the compiled-in policy: outbound resolve, TCP, and
UDP over both address families permitted, internal destinations denied, and at
most 16 resolver results per session.
The entry schema is closed \(em an unknown key, a non-boolean value, or an
invalid or duplicate label makes the file malformed \(em but the file-level
failure direction is fail-soft: a missing or malformed file leaves the
compiled-in default policy in force and logs a warning, so a bad
configuration can never take the network provider down with it.
The applied policy source
.Pq admin, label, or default
is logged at each session start.
.Sh SECURITY
Session creation and resolver policy decisions are recorded through OpenBSM.
Session, resolution, connect, and rejection paths expose DTrace probes through
the
.Dq bsdnetwork_provider
provider.
The
.Sy connect-done
probe reports the client label, the
.Dq addr:port
dialed, the requested timeout in milliseconds
.Pq zero for a blocking connect ,
and the errno-style outcome
.Pq zero connected, Er ETIMEDOUT No on expiry .
.Sh NETWORK STATE
The separately gated
.Va system.Network.State
endpoint requires
.Va system.network.observe
and read session right bit 1.
It publishes sampled interface index, name, flags, MTU, and link state,
with an epoch, generation, and explicit source error.
It exposes neither addresses nor packet counters.
A shared sampler reads BSDSysctl approximately one second after each read;
identical samples are suppressed and slow watchers receive the latest state.
Discovery, wire negotiation, reconnect and interface sampling share one
one-second libservice RPC budget per iteration.
A stalled dependency therefore cannot accumulate separate RPC wait limits
before the sampler checks for shutdown.
This budget bounds RPC waits, not scheduling delays, mutex acquisition or
arbitrary kernel work.
A failed read publishes unavailable state with zero records, then recovery
publishes a new snapshot.
This does not indicate Internet reachability.
.Pp
The provider uses only the BSDSysctl read grant for
.Va net.routetable ;
it receives no sysctl token or write permission.
Existing sysctl isolation remains unchanged.
State sessions stay in the parent reactor, with at most 128 sessions,
16 per authenticated label, and one outstanding watch per session.
A 30-second idle limit applies between requests.
See
.Xr network_state 3
for timeout and reconnect semantics.
.Sh CONCURRENT DNS REQUESTS
Each session runs one resolver job at a time.
Up to 16 byte-identical requests may share that job and receive separate
replies on their original channel transactions.
Different overlapping requests and additional duplicates are refused with
.Er EBUSY .
Joining an existing job does not extend its original deadline.
Coalescing never crosses sessions or authenticated client labels.
A failed reply terminates the session and releases all retained requests.
.Sh DNS CACHE AND TTL
Each connection worker retains at most 32 validated positive or negative DNS
answers,
keyed by hostname and record type, for at most five minutes and never beyond
the minimum TTL of the matching addresses and their CNAME chain.
The cache is private to that worker and its immutable client policy;
answers are not shared across sessions, labels, or network namespaces.
NXDOMAIN and NODATA responses are cached only with a usable IN-class SOA
covering the terminal name in the CNAME chain.
Their lifetime is the minimum of the SOA TTL, SOA MINIMUM, CNAME lifetimes,
and five minutes.
Negative entries remain keyed by both hostname and record type; they do not
suppress queries for another type or descendant names.
Missing, malformed, zero-lifetime, or high-bit SOA lifetimes are not cached.
Temporary server and transport failures are not cached.
Numeric and local hosts results are resolved before consulting DNS.
.Pp
Every query checks the nameserver snapshot and its generation.
A change clears the worker's cache, including when the server list later
returns to an earlier value.
An in-flight query may finish against its original configuration, but the
next query invalidates its cached result after a generation change.
.Pp
The reply TTL is the conservative whole-second remaining minimum across
returned address families and CNAMEs; it accounts for elapsed time and cache
age.
Zero means no positive cache lifetime is advertised, including local results.
.Sh DNS FAILURES
When no usable address is available, an unsuccessful DNS query returns
.Er EAGAIN
through the broker protocol, or
.Er EAI_AGAIN
from
.Fn networkcmp_getaddrinfo .
Complete negative answers return
.Er ENOENT
or
.Er EAI_NONAME ,
respectively.
If one queried family fails and another returns only a negative answer,
the result remains retryable.
A usable address from either family, including IPv4-mapped IPv6 fallback,
can still satisfy the lookup.
Numeric addresses do not require a working nameserver.
.Pp
.Dv AI_V4MAPPED
with
.Dv AF_INET6
also maps numeric IPv4 literals and matching local hosts entries.
A numeric literal with
.Dv AI_NUMERICHOST
never requires DNS.
Native IPv6 hosts entries take precedence unless
.Dv AI_ALL
requests both native and mapped entries.
A matching hosts entry satisfies the lookup before DNS is attempted.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libnetworkcmp 3 ,
.Xr network_state 3 ,
.Xr fidl 7 ,
.Xr switchboard 8
